      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm trying to run this macro for our monthly reconciliation but i keep getting a \"run-time error '1004': application-defined or object-defined error\" on this line:\n\nCells(i, 4).Value = wsRecon.Range(\"B\" & i).Value\n\ncan u tell me why this is happening? here is the sub:\n\nSub MonthlyRecon()\n    Dim wsRecon As Worksheet\n    Dim i As Integer\n    Set wsRecon = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Recon_Data_Final\")\n    \n    For i = 2 To 500\n        Cells(i, 4).Value = wsRecon.Range(\"B\" & i).Value\n    Next i\nEnd Sub",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A \"Run-time error '1004'\" in VBA, especially when dealing with cell assignments, often means the code is trying to interact with something that isn't properly defined or accessible. It's much more likely that the issue lies in how you are referencing the cells, not necessarily the loop itself. You should always be explicit about *which* worksheet `Cells(i, 4)` belongs to, otherwise, it defaults to the active sheet, which might not be your intended worksheet.\n\nTo fix this, change `Cells(i, 4)` to `wsRecon.Cells(i, 4)`. This ensures you are writing the value directly into column D of your \"Recon\\_Data\\_Final\" sheet, keeping all your operations contained within the correct worksheet object.\n\nHere is the revised snippet:\n\n```vba\nSub MonthlyRecon()\n    Dim wsRecon As Worksheet\n    Dim i As Integer\n    Set wsRecon = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Recon_Data_Final\")\n    \n    For i = 2 To 500\n        wsRecon.Cells(i, 4).Value = wsRecon.Range(\"",
